## Phase 5 — Final Diagnosis

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR `zebra` daemon (routing manager / kernel-interface broker) and its companion watchdog process `watchfrr` have both died on `super_spine_router_0`. Only `bgpd` remains alive.

**Mechanism:** In FRR's architecture, `zebra` is the central broker: it manages interface state, the kernel routing table (FIB), and provides the shared RIB that all protocol daemons query. The `vtysh` CLI connects to each daemon in turn; when `zebra` is absent, any vtysh command that touches the route table or interface state (`show ip route`, `show interface`) fails with a daemon-connection error. `bgpd`, however, maintains its own BGP finite-state machine and TCP sessions independently of `zebra` — so both spine sessions remain Established even after zebra crashes. The kernel FIB, programmed by `zebra` before it died, still holds the forwarding entries, which is why `pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1` traffic flows normally. `watchfrr`'s absence means there is no watchdog to detect the crash and restart `zebra`.

**Localization:** `super_spine_router_0` — confirmed by `ps aux` showing only `/usr/lib/frr/bgpd` running; `zebra` and `watchfrr` have no entries in the process table. All four peer routers (both spines, both leaf routers) show the full `watchfrr=yes / zebra=yes / bgpd=yes` triplet.

**Root cause:** The `zebra` and `watchfrr` FRR daemons crashed (or were killed) on `super_spine_router_0`, leaving `bgpd` as the sole surviving FRR process.

**Fix:** Restart the FRR service on `super_spine_router_0` via `systemctl restart frr`. This will bring `watchfrr`, `zebra`, and all configured daemons back up together; `bgpd` will re-synchronize the RIB with `zebra`, and FRR CLI queries will resume normally.
=== END ===